.\"	@(#)sigprocmask.2	8/16/92
.\"
.TH SIGPROCMASK 2P "August 1, 1992"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
sigprocmask \- (POSIX only) examine or change the signal mask of the calling process.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
#include <signal.h>
.PP
.B sigprocmask(int \fIhow\fP, const sigset_t *\fIset\fP, 
.B sigset_t *\fIoset\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The information in this specification applies
only to POSIX applications.
.PP
The
.I sigprocmask
function is used to examine or change (or both) the signal mask of
the calling process. If the value of the argument \fIset\fR is not
\fBNULL\fR, it points to a set of signals to be used to change the
currently blocked set.
.PP
The value of the argument \fIhow\fR indicates the manner in which 
the set is changed.  The following values are accepted:
.TP 15
SIG_BLOCK
The resulting set shall be the union of the current set and the
signal set pointed to by the argument \fIset\fR.
.TP 15
SIG_UNBLOCK
The resulting set shall be the intersection of the current set and
the complement of the signal set pointed to by the argument \fIset\fR.
.TP 15
SIG_SETMASK
The resulting set shall be the signal set ponted to by the argument \fIset\fR.
.PP
If the argument \fIoset\fR is not \fBNULL\fR, the previous mask is
stored in the space pointed to by \fIoset\fR.  If the value of the
argument \fIset\fR is \fBNULL\fR, the value of the argument \fIhow\fR
is not significant and the signal mask of the process is unchanged.
.PP
If there are any pending unblocked signals after the call to 
.I sigprocmask
at least one of those signals shall be delivered before 
.I sigprocmask 
returns.
.PP
It is not possible to block the SIGKILL and SIGSTOP signals.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion a value of zero is returned.  Otherwise, a value
of \-1 is returned and \fIerrno\fR is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The value of the \fIhow\fR argument is not equal to one of the defined values.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sigaction(2P), sigpending(2P),  sigsuspend(2P), sigsetops(3P)
